WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.588
I ask pi to check the repeated

00:02.588 --> 00:04.877
code in this project before making a

00:04.877 --> 00:07.165
change. It calls Deslop and brings the

00:07.165 --> 00:09.454
result into the conversation. I start with

00:09.454 --> 00:11.742
one finding so the review stays small

00:11.742 --> 00:13.704
enough to understand and act on.

00:14.504 --> 00:16.788
I ask the agent for the files

00:16.788 --> 00:19.072
behind that finding. The reply shows where

00:19.072 --> 00:21.356
the repeated code lives and how many

00:21.356 --> 00:23.639
copies were found. I use those locations

00:23.639 --> 00:25.923
to guide the next step, rather than

00:25.923 --> 00:28.207
asking for a broad rewrite of the

00:28.207 --> 00:28.534
project.

00:29.334 --> 00:31.812
Before adding another helper, I ask pi

00:31.812 --> 00:34.291
to read the existing file and search

00:34.291 --> 00:36.770
for similar code. Deslop returns the matching

00:36.770 --> 00:39.249
files. This is the useful moment to

00:39.249 --> 00:41.728
spot something I can reuse, while there

00:41.728 --> 00:44.206
is still time to avoid writing another

00:44.206 --> 00:44.561
copy.

00:45.361 --> 00:47.832
I finish by opening the source comparison

00:47.832 --> 00:50.304
in the editor. I check that the

00:50.304 --> 00:52.775
proposed reuse preserves the behavior I need.

00:52.775 --> 00:55.247
After a focused change, I run the

00:55.247 --> 00:57.719
project's tests and review Deslop again instead

00:57.719 --> 01:00.190
of assuming the agent's first answer is

01:00.190 --> 01:00.543
enough.
